          "parts": [
            {
              "text": "When wrapping an existing ERP with the Microsoft Power Platform (avoiding a costly rip-and-replace), you need a partner that understands **manufacturing operational tech (OT/IT)**, **ERP integration patterns** (APIs, SQL connectors, or RPA), and **shop-floor UX** (offline mode, ruggedised tablets, barcode scanning).\n\nRecommended regional (North East) and UK-wide consultancies with verified capabilities in manufacturing and Power Platform include:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Regional North East Specialists (Local to Newcastle)\n\n* **Waterstons (Durham / Newcastle)**\n  * **Why them:** Headquartered just down the road in Durham, Waterstons is one of the premier business and technology consultancies in the North East. They have a dedicated manufacturing practice and frequently work on \"ERP optimisation\" rather than forced system replacement.\n  * **Best for:** Complex architectures, integrating with mid-market legacy ERPs (Syspro, EFACS, Sage, Epicor), and building out robust Power Platform workflows and automation.\n* **Monpellier (Gateshead)**\n  * **Why them:** Located right across the Tyne, Monpellier specializes in manufacturing ERPs (Dynamics, Sage) and builds bespoke Power Apps for barcode scanning, inventory movements, and shop-floor data capture (SFDC).\n  * **Best for:** Practical, rapid shop-floor tablet/scanner rollouts and small-to-mid-sized manufacturing operations.\n* **SR1 Development (Sunderland / Newcastle)**\n  * **Why them:** A local boutique Microsoft low-code development firm. \n  * **Best for:** Tactical, fast-turnaround Power Automate and Power Apps buildouts if you have internal IT overseeing the ERP database.\n\n---\n\n### 2. UK Specialists with Dedicated Manufacturing Practices\n\n* **Bridgeall**\n  * **Why them:** They have an explicit practice dedicated to *UK manufacturers modernising shop-floor data collection without replacing their ERP*. They regularly deliver paperless quality audit apps, OEE tracking, and digital routing sheets that feed back-office databases.\n  * **Best for:** Shop-floor data capture, scrap/downtime tracking, and connecting front-line plant workers to central systems.\n* **Tecman (Technology Management)**\n  * **Why them:** A heavyweight Microsoft partner hyper-focused on UK manufacturing, distribution, and supply chains. They specialize in shop-floor data capture, warehouse mobility, and automated production order routing.\n  * **Best for:** In-depth manufacturing routing, Bills of Materials (BOM) logic, and operational automation.\n* **Columbus Global UK**\n  * **Why them:** An international enterprise partner with a large UK footprint that specializes almost entirely in manufacturing and food production. They excel at building Power Platform \"wrappers\" and Azure integration pipelines around legacy or on-premise ERPs.\n  * **Best for:** Larger, multi-site manufacturers with high transaction volumes and complex compliance requirements.\n\n---\n\n### How to Structure Your Conversations (Questions to Ask)\n\nWhen engaging these partners, evaluate them against these manufacturing-specific criteria:\n\n1. **ERP Connector Strategy:**\n   * *Ask:* *\"How do you plan to interface with our specific ERP without corrupting business logic?\"*\n   * *What good looks like:* If your ERP has REST/SOAP APIs, they should propose **custom Power Platform connectors** or **Azure Logic Apps**. If it is a legacy SQL-based system with no API, they should discuss read-only data staging or audited stored procedures. If there is no backend access, they should discuss **Power Automate Desktop (RPA)**.\n2. **Order Processing Automation:**\n   * *Ask:* *\"How do you handle unstructured purchase orders sent via email or PDF?\"*\n   * *What good looks like:* Using **Power Automate + AI Builder** (Document Processing) to parse customer POs, extract line items, match SKUs against your ERP master data, and route them for one-click approval before writing into the sales order table.\n3. **Shop-Floor Device & Network Realities:**\n   * *Ask:* *\"How do your Power Apps handle barcode scanning, glove use, and Wi-Fi dead zones on the factory floor?\"*\n   * *What good looks like:* The partner should have experience building Canvas Apps with **offline caching**, high-contrast UI, integration with rugged Android Zebra/Honeywell scanners (via hardware intent or camera wedge), and simple touch targets.\n4. **Licensing Architecture:**\n   * *Ask:* *\"How will you keep Microsoft licensing costs low for 50+ shop-floor machine operators?\"*\n   * *What good looks like:* Shop-floor data entry should not necessarily require a full £15–£30/user/month Power Apps license for every operator. A smart partner will propose shared kiosk devices, Microsoft 365 frontline worker licenses, or staging data through intermediate endpoints to keep ongoing licensing overhead down.",
              "thoughtSignature": ""
            }
